{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\user\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Microsoft\\\\WindowsApps\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\\\\Projects\\\\Agent_Workspace\\\\HG-KSEOS\\\\var\\\\swof-construction-002-w2-repair-001\\\\specs\\\\R2_WO-SWOF-W2-R002_SPEC.md' -Raw\"","aggregated_output":"# R2 WO-SWOF-W2-R002 — close the fresh-checker findings on the token seam and the HumanGate route\n\nrepair_id: SWOF-W2-CLOSURE-R1 (corrective round R2)\norder_id: SWOF-CONSTRUCTION-002-W2-REPAIR-001\nbase: the R1 candidate (see the writer prompt for the exact SHA)\nsource: findings raised by the fresh-context VERIFY_ONLY checkers on the R1 candidate\n\n## 0. Why this round exists\n\nThe R1 checkers confirmed both external defects are closed, and then found defects that R1 INTRODUCED or\nleft open. Treat each as an acceptance edge, not as polish.\n\n```\nBLK-1  TOK-INV-010 is only partially implemented. token.signature is regex-shape-checked and never\n       cryptographically verified. ctx.trusted_key_registry is called as\n       registry(issuer_id, key_id, key_generation) - it receives no signature and no payload, so by\n       construction it can only assert KEY TRUST. A forged shape-valid signature is APPROVED whenever\n       the key triple is trusted. The R1 spec's claim that \"signature verification is delegated to an\n       injected trusted key registry\" was therefore inaccurate; this round makes the delegation REAL.\nBLK-2  A token with approver == actor passes when the caller omits requesting_actor: the verifier never\n       compares approver to actor, and the wrapper only compares to the OPTIONAL requesting_actor.\nBLK-3  assert_human_gate_satisfied trusts the HumanGateRoute it is handed: a directly constructed\n       HumanGateRoute(required=False) skips the gate for a high-risk action.\nNB-1   DENY_P3_TOKEN_REQUIRED (TOK-INV-001) is never emitted.\nNB-2   key_generation staleness is skipped when ctx.key_generation is None.\n```\n\n## 1. Files you may write (exactly these, nothing else)\n\n```\nsrc/security/humangate.py                          (modify)\nsrc/security/rights.py                             (modify)\nsrc/security/tests/test_humangate_currentness.py   (modify: add the cases in section 5)\nsrc/security/tests/test_rights.py                  (modify only if a case genuinely needs it)\n```\n\nPython standard library only. ASCII only. LF line endings. Do NOT implement or import any cryptography\nlibrary. Do NOT touch any other path.\n\n## 2. BLK-1 — make the signature delegation real\n\n### 2.1 Add a canonical payload function\n\n```python\ndef canonical_payload_bytes(token) -> bytes:\n    \"\"\"The bytes a real verifier signs: UTF-8 JSON of the token EXCLUDING `signature`, with keys sorted\n    and separators (',', ':') - i.e. a deterministic canonical form. The library performs NO\n    cryptography; it only defines the bytes so a real verifier can recompute them.\"\"\"\n```\nImplementation: `json.dumps({k: v for k, v in asdict(token).items() if k != \"signature\"}, sort_keys=True,\nseparators=(\",\", \":\"), ensure_ascii=False).encode(\"utf-8\")`. Tuples serialise as lists; that is fine as\nlong as it is deterministic.\n\n### 2.2 Change the registry contract to a VERIFIER\n\n`VerificationContext.trusted_key_registry` must now be called as:\n\n```python\nok = ctx.trusted_key_registry(payload_bytes, token.signature, token.issuer_id, token.key_id,\n                              token.key_generation)\n```\n\nand must return exactly `True` to be accepted. Document in the docstring that:\n\n- the registry is the ONLY place a signature may be judged, and it MUST recompute a signature over\n  `payload_bytes` with the named key (or verify it against a trust store) and return a strict bool;\n- the library itself never claims to have verified a signature;\n- a registry that only checks key existence is NOT a valid registry and will accept forgeries — that is\n  a contract violation by the integrator, and the failure mode is documented here explicitly.\n\nFailure rules, all `DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY`, all fail closed:\n\n```\nctx.trusted_key_registry is None                       -> DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY\nregistry returns anything that is not exactly True     -> DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY\nregistry raises                                        -> DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY (catch Exception)\nregistry has an incompatible arity (TypeError)         -> DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY\n```\n\nAdd `def registry_signature_verifier(...)`? NO - do not add a second mechanism. One verifier, one path.\n\n## 3. BLK-2 — approver must not be the actor\n\nIn `verify_approval_token`, after the false-authority check, add:\n\n```\ntoken.approver.strip() == token.actor.strip()   -> DENY_FALSE_AUTHORITY\n```\n\n(The request-bound `actor` and the token's `actor` are already required to match by exact binding, so\ncomparing `token.approver` to `token.actor` is equivalent to comparing it to the proposer.)\nKeep the wrapper's existing `SELF_APPROVAL_REFUSED` behaviour for an explicit `requesting_actor`.\n\n## 4. BLK-3 — the route must be re-derived, never trusted\n\nIn `assert_human_gate_satisfied(route, approval, *, requesting_actor=None, request=None, ctx=None)`:\n\n```\nif not isinstance(route, HumanGateRoute):            raise UNKNOWN_ROUTE_FAIL_CLOSED   (unchanged)\nif request is not None:\n    derived = human_gate_route(request.operation)\n    if derived.required and not route.required:      raise ROUTE_UNDERSTATES_ACTION\n    if derived.required != route.required:           raise ROUTE_UNDERSTATES_ACTION\nif not route.required:\n    if request is None:                              raise ROUTE_NOT_REDERIVABLE_FAIL_CLOSED\n    return\n... existing typed-token path ...\n```\n\nSo a `required=False` route is only honoured when a `request` was supplied and re-derivation agrees.\nA gated route now also surfaces the canonical first-fail code for TOK-INV-001: if `request` is supplied\nand `(request.effect_risk_tier in {\"P3\",\"P4\",\"P5\"} or request.permission_class in {\"P3\",\"P4\",\"P5\"})`\nand the approval is not a valid typed token, raise `HumanGateBypassAttempt(\"DENY_P3_TOKEN_REQUIRED\")`\ninstead of `NOT_A_CANONICAL_APPROVAL_TOKEN`.\n\n## 5. Tests you MUST add (acceptance evidence)\n\n```\n1  forged signature: shape-valid 86-char signature, registry that returns True WITHOUT consulting the\n   payload, and a token whose payload does NOT correspond to the signature -> the library accepts only\n   because the registry is a contract-violating stub; assert and DOCUMENT this as the integrator's\n   failure mode. (This is a documentation test; keep it clearly named.)\n2  honest registry: a verifier that recomputes an HMAC/sha256 over canonical_payload_bytes with a test\n   key and returns a strict bool -> a matching token PASSES (APPROVE_BASIS_SATISFIED)\n3  honest registry + tampered payload (change subject_hash after signing) -> DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY\n4  honest registry + wrong key_generation -> DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY\n5  registry returns None / False / raises / has wrong arity -> DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY each\n6  registry is None -> DENY_TOKEN_INTEGRITY\n7  approver == actor -> DENY_FALSE_AUTHORITY\n8  route required=False + request present for a HIGH-RISK operation -> ROUTE_UNDERSTATES_ACTION\n9  route required=False + request is None -> ROUTE_NOT_REDERIVABLE_FAIL_CLOSED\n10 route required=False + request present for a genuinely low-risk operation -> returns None\n11 P3 request with a bare string approval -> DENY_P3_TOKEN_REQUIRED\n12 P3 request with a valid typed token -> passes\n13 canonical_payload_bytes is deterministic across two calls and differs when any field differs\n14 the existing positive control (fully valid current exact-bound token) still PASSES\n```\n\nEvery previously passing test must still pass. Do not delete or weaken any existing case; the\ndenominator may only grow.\n\n## 6. Absolute rules\n\n- Fail closed. No cryptography implementation, no new dependency, no network.\n- Never read the wall clock.\n- Do not weaken any check that R1 added.\n- Keep every public name in `src/security/rights.py` and `src/security/humangate.py` importable.\n\n## 7. When finished print exactly one JSON object\n\n```json\n{\"files\": [\"<every path you wrote>\"], \"test_command\": \"<the command you ran>\",\n \"test_result\": \"<stdout tail>\", \"denominator\": <int tests collected in src/security/tests>}\n```\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
